## Code Patterns

### Controller (HTML-first, JSON-aware)

```ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

class ProfilesController < ApplicationController
  before_action :authenticate_user!
  before_action :set_profile, only: %i[show edit update destroy]
  before_action :authorize_profile!, only: %i[edit update destroy]

  # GET /profiles
  def index
    @profiles = current_user.profiles
      .includes(:company, :tags)
      .page(params[:page]).per(25)

    respond_to do |format|
      format.html
      format.json { render json: @profiles.map { |p| ProfilePresenter.new(p).call } }
    end
  end

  # GET /profiles/:id
  def show
    respond_to do |format|
      format.html
      format.json { render json: ProfilePresenter.new(@profile).call }
    end
  end

  # GET /profiles/new
  def new
    @profile = current_user.profiles.build
  end

  # POST /profiles
  def create
    @profile = current_user.profiles.build(profile_params)

    if @profile.save
      respond_to do |format|
        format.html { redirect_to @profile, notice: 'Profile created.' }
        format.json { render json: ProfilePresenter.new(@profile).call, status: :created }
      end
    else
      respond_to do |format|
        format.html { render :new, status: :unprocessable_entity }
        format.json { render json: { errors: @profile.errors }, status: :unprocessable_entity }
      end
    end
  end

  # PATCH/PUT /profiles/:id
  def update
    if @profile.update(profile_params)
      respond_to do |format|
        format.html { redirect_to @profile, notice: 'Profile updated.' }
        format.json { render json: ProfilePresenter.new(@profile).call }
      end
    else
      respond_to do |format|
        format.html { render :edit, status: :unprocessable_entity }
        format.json { render json: { errors: @profile.errors }, status: :unprocessable_entity }
      end
    end
  end

  # DELETE /profiles/:id
  def destroy
    @profile.destroy!
    respond_to do |format|
      format.html { redirect_to profiles_path, notice: 'Profile deleted.', status: :see_other }
      format.json { head :no_content }
    end
  end

  private

  def set_profile
    @profile = current_user.profiles.find(params[:id])
  end

  def authorize_profile!
    head :forbidden unless @profile.user == current_user
  end

  def profile_params
    params.expect(profile: [:name, :email, :bio, :company_id])
  end
end
```

Key differences from API-only Rails:
- Instance variables (`@profile`) for HTML views (not local `profile`)
- `respond_to` blocks for HTML + JSON
- `redirect_to` with flash on success (HTML)
- `render :new, status: :unprocessable_entity` on validation failure ({{ 'Turbo expects 422' if v.turbo else 'HTTP 422, the Rails 7+ convention' }})
- `status: :see_other` on destroy redirect ({{ 'Turbo requirement for DELETE' if v.turbo else 'so browsers follow with GET' }})

### Service Pattern (unchanged from API)

```ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

module Users
  class SyncProfileService < ApplicationService
    def initialize(user:, provider:)
      @user = user
      @provider = provider
    end

    def call
      return if user.synced_recently?

      sync_from_provider
    end

    private

    attr_reader :user, :provider

    def sync_from_provider
      # ...
    end
  end
end
```

### Helper Pattern

Helpers expose presentation logic to views. Keep them small and view-only.

```ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

module ProfilesHelper
  # @param profile [Profile]
  # @return [ActiveSupport::SafeBuffer] avatar HTML
  def profile_avatar(profile, size: :md)
    image_tag profile.avatar_url(size: size),
              class: '{{ v.css.avatar }}',
              alt: profile.name
  end

  # @param status [String]
  # @return [String] CSS class for the status badge
  def profile_status_class(status)
    case status
{% if v.ui == 'tailwind' %}
    when 'active'   then 'bg-green-100 text-green-800'
    when 'inactive' then 'bg-gray-100 text-gray-800'
    when 'archived' then 'bg-red-100 text-red-800'
{% elif v.ui == 'bootstrap' %}
    when 'active'   then 'badge text-bg-success'
    when 'inactive' then 'badge text-bg-secondary'
    when 'archived' then 'badge text-bg-danger'
{% else %}
    when 'active'   then 'badge badge--active'
    when 'inactive' then 'badge badge--inactive'
    when 'archived' then 'badge badge--archived'
{% endif %}
    end
  end
end
```

Rules:
- Helpers are PRESENTATION ONLY — no DB queries, no business logic
- Move complex view logic into {{ 'ViewComponents' if v.vc else ('Phlex components' if v.phlex else 'partials or presenters') }} instead
- Document parameters with YARD

### Model, Job, Migration patterns

Identical to API Rails — the core patterns file listed above has the details.
The monolith does NOT change how models, jobs, or migrations are written.
{% if variables.orm == 'Mongoid' %}
Models are Mongoid documents (`include Mongoid::Document`, `field`, `index`), not
ActiveRecord: there are no migrations; read `.claude/patterns/{{ 'patterns/mongoid.md' | installed(stack) }}`.
{% endif %}
Jobs use ActiveJob's `retry_on` / `discard_on` / `queue_as` on every adapter;
{% if (variables.job_adapter | default('solid_queue')) == 'sidekiq' %}
`sidekiq_options` is available because the adapter is Sidekiq, but prefer the ActiveJob API.
{% else %}
never call `sidekiq_options` (it raises `NoMethodError` on {{ variables.background_jobs }}).
{% endif %}

### Health Route

Auto-verify polls `GET /health` on port {{ variables.dev_port }} (started with `bin/dev`).
A fresh Rails 8 app only routes `/up`, so keep this alias in `config/routes.rb`:

```ruby
get 'up' => 'rails/health#show', as: :rails_health_check
get 'health' => 'rails/health#show', as: :health_check
```

## Style Rules (MANDATORY)

1. **`frozen_string_literal: true`** - First line of every Ruby file
2. **Quotes per `.rubocop.yml`** - Rails 8's `rubocop-rails-omakase` means double quotes; match the config so `rubocop -A` doesn't rewrite your code
3. **Hash shorthand** - `{ id:, name: }` not `{ id: id, name: name }`
4. **Guard clauses** - `return if condition?` instead of nested conditionals
5. **YARD docs** - On every public method (helpers especially benefit from this)
6. **`includes()`** - Always eager-load associations to prevent N+1 queries
7. **Strong params** - `params.expect(model: [:attr, ...])` (Rails 8); never `params.permit!`
8. **Instance vars in controllers** - Set `@variable` for HTML views; views read them
9. **`status: :unprocessable_entity` on form errors** - {{ 'Turbo expects 422 to re-render' if v.turbo else 'browsers and Rails 7+ conventions expect 422' }}
10. **`status: :see_other` on DELETE redirects** - {{ 'Turbo expects 303' if v.turbo else 'the browser follows with GET' }}
11. **Auth** - Use the app's existing auth; when there is none, `bin/rails generate authentication` (Rails 8) over Devise (see `.claude/patterns/{{ 'patterns/auth.md' | installed(stack) }}`)

